1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
Section 2

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,763
Good morning.

3
00:00:03,763 --> 00:00:04,962
My name's Lucy Crittenden,

4
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:11,405
and l'm the Director of Operations for an organisation that arranges volunteering in this

5
00:00:11,405 --> 00:00:13,246
part of the country.

6
00:00:13,246 --> 00:00:19,690
I'm hoping I can persuade one or two of you to become volunteers yourselves.

7
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:24,740
Let me start by briefly explaining what we mean by volunteering.

8
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:30,800
Volunteers are teenagers and adults who choose to spend some time, unpaid, helping other

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,531
people in some way.

10
00:00:32,531 --> 00:00:37,293
Most volunteers devote two or three hours to this every week,

11
00:00:37,293 --> 00:00:39,890
while a few do much more.

12
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:45,500
The people they help may have physical or behavioural difficulties, for example.

13
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:51,176
Volunteers can do all sorts of things, depending on their own abilities and interests.

14
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:56,852
If they're supporting a family that's struggling, for example, they may be able to

15
00:00:56,852 --> 00:00:58,879
give them tips on cooking,

16
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:04,555
or recommend how to plan their budget or how to shop sensibly on their

17
00:01:04,555 --> 00:01:04,960
income.

18
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:10,472
They might even do some painting or wallpapering, perhaps alongside any members of the

19
00:01:10,472 --> 00:01:13,228
family who are able to do it.

20
00:01:13,228 --> 00:01:18,740
Or even do some babysitting so that parents can go out for a while.

21
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:23,601
The benefit from volunteering isn't only for the people being helped.

22
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:25,811
Volunteers also gain from it:

23
00:01:25,811 --> 00:01:31,556
they're using their skills to cope with somebody's mental or physical ill health,

24
00:01:31,556 --> 00:01:37,743
and volunteering may be a valuable element of their CV when they're applying for

25
00:01:37,743 --> 00:01:38,185
jobs:

26
00:01:38,185 --> 00:01:43,930
employers usually look favourably on someone who's given up time to help others.

27
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:50,424
Significantly, most volunteers feel that what they're doing gives them a purpose in their

28
00:01:50,424 --> 00:01:50,888
lives.

29
00:01:50,888 --> 00:01:57,382
And in my opinion, they're lucky in that respect, as many people don't have

30
00:01:57,382 --> 00:01:58,310
that feeling.

31
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:03,032
Now I'd like to tell you what some of our volunteers have said about

32
00:02:03,032 --> 00:02:07,754
what they do, to give you an idea of the range of ways in

33
00:02:07,754 --> 00:02:09,440
which they can help people.

34
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:14,627
Habib supports an elderly lady who's beginning to show signs of dementia.

35
00:02:14,627 --> 00:02:20,678
Once a week they, along with other elderly people, go to the local community

36
00:02:20,678 --> 00:02:25,000
centre, where a group of people come in and sing.

37
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,780
The songs take the listeners back to their youth,

38
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:34,660
and for a little while they can forget the difficulties that they face now.

39
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:38,024
Our volunteer Consuela is an amazing woman.

40
00:02:38,024 --> 00:02:40,427
She has difficulty walking herself,

41
00:02:40,427 --> 00:02:43,791
but she doesn't let that stop her.

42
00:02:43,791 --> 00:02:48,116
She helps a couple of people with similar difficulties,

43
00:02:48,116 --> 00:02:51,000
who had almost stopped walking altogether.

44
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:59,440
By using herself as an example, Consuela encourages them to walk more and more.

45
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:05,829
Minh visits a young man who lives alone and can't leave his home on

46
00:03:05,829 --> 00:03:06,742
his own,

47
00:03:06,742 --> 00:03:09,480
so he hardly ever saw anyone.

48
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,423
But together they go out to the cinema,

49
00:03:12,423 --> 00:03:17,574
or to see friends the young man hadn't been able to visit for a

50
00:03:17,574 --> 00:03:18,310
long time.

51
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:22,281
Tanya visits an elderly woman once a week.

52
00:03:22,281 --> 00:03:29,230
When the woman found out that Tanya is a professional dressmaker, she got interested.

53
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:33,460
Tanya showed her some soft toys she'd made,

54
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:37,690
and the woman decided to try it herself.

55
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:41,470
And now she really enjoys it,

56
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:44,620
and spends hours making toys.

57
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:48,193
They're not perhaps up to Tanya's standard yet,

58
00:03:48,193 --> 00:03:52,660
but she gains a lot of pleasure from doing it.

59
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:59,680
Alexei is a volunteer with a family that faces a number of difficulties.

60
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:06,038
By calmly talking over possible solutions with family members, he's helping them to realise

61
00:04:06,038 --> 00:04:07,855
that they aren't helpless,

62
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:12,851
and that they can do something themselves to improve their situation.

63
00:04:12,851 --> 00:04:16,030
This has been great for their self-esteem.

64
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:22,157
And the last volunteer I'll mention, though there are plenty more, is Juba.

65
00:04:22,157 --> 00:04:26,398
She volunteers with a teenage girl with learning difficulties,

66
00:04:26,398 --> 00:04:30,640
who wasn't very good at talking to other people.

67
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,907
Juba's worked very patiently with her,

68
00:04:33,907 --> 00:04:39,352
and now the girl is far better at expressing herself,

69
00:04:39,352 --> 00:04:41,530
and understanding other people.

70
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:48,934
OK, I hope that's given you an idea of what volunteering is all about.

71
00:04:48,934 --> 00:04:50,520
Now I'd like ...
